{% import 'chrome/macros.html.j2' as c %}
<section>
  <h2>How This Was Measured</h2>

  <p>Every figure in this report comes from one of two sources, and they are never mixed.
  <strong>Measured</strong> figures were produced by a probe run against real browser engines
  on this hardware. <strong>Documented</strong> figures come from vendor material, and are
  labelled wherever they appear.</p>

  <h3>The measurement</h3>

  <table class="kv">
    <tr>
      <td>One operation per browser</td>
      <td>Each measurement launches a fresh browser with a fresh profile, performs exactly one
          operation at one file size, and exits. Nothing can inherit memory from a previous
          operation, because there is no previous operation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Three repetitions</td>
      <td>Every cell is measured three times. Tables report the median with the observed range,
          so the reader can see how stable a figure is rather than trusting a single number.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>A control</td>
      <td>A run that allocates nothing, measured identically. Whatever it reports is the noise
          floor: Chromium 1 MiB, Firefox 8 MiB, WebKit under zero. No result below roughly
          10 MiB is treated as meaningful.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The real download path</td>
      <td>The file is prepared, then handed to the browser through an ordinary download link,
          and the browser's own download machinery is allowed to complete it. Cost is measured
          across that whole operation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Memory from outside</td>
      <td>Resident memory of the entire browser process tree, sampled twenty-five times a
          second from outside the browser, attributed against the memory in use immediately
          before each operation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Identical conditions</td>
      <td>All three engines run in the same container image, on the same host, with profiles on
          the same real disk. No engine is special-cased.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  {{ c.warn('One engine could not be measured',
     'The WebKit build available for automated testing exposes no storage interface at all —
      not merely the capability under test, but the entire interface, in a secure context,
      under every configuration tried. Real Safari has it. This is a property of that build,
      which is not Safari. Every Safari row in this report is therefore documented rather than
      measured, and is marked as such.') }}

  <h3>What the measurement does not cover</h3>

  <p>All measurement was on desktop Linux; no macOS, no Windows, no mobile hardware. The sweep
  stops at 2 GiB because the test machine has 8 GiB free and a larger case would measure the
  host rather than the browser. Storage quota figures belong to the machine that produced them
  and imply nothing about a user's computer. Resident memory is coarse and includes memory not
  yet reclaimed, so the transient peak is reported — it is the figure that exhausts a device.</p>
</section>
